= Requested knowledge =

This page records important knowledge BITwiki should cover or strengthen without creating empty Main-namespace shells first.

'''Requested ≠ canonical.''' A request identifies a coverage need. It becomes durable knowledge only after research, sourcing, modeling, writing, and review.

The queue is now composed from [[Template:Knowledge request]]. Each visible record is also stored in Cargo table <code>Knowledge_requests</code>, making lifecycle state queryable without duplicating a second hidden backlog.

== Why use a request queue? ==

An empty or generic article hides the difference between ''we have this knowledge'' and ''we intend to research this knowledge.'' A request queue keeps that distinction explicit.

Use this page when:

* a topic is clearly important but no coherent sourced page exists yet;
* a current page is too weak to count as meaningful coverage;
* a portal/category reveals a missing prerequisite or bridge topic;
* a Core/Vital coverage review finds an important gap;
* a domain expansion needs evidence before new schema is introduced.

== Request record ==

For each request, record only what is known:

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Meaning
|-
| Proposed title || Likely canonical title; may change after research
|-
| Why it matters || Coverage/navigation/research reason
|-
| Candidate Domain(s) || Existing likely fields; do not invent a Domain to satisfy the request
|-
| Source leads || Strong starting sources, when known
|-
| Needed depth || Simple / Core / Technical / Frontier / full page, or scope research when the request itself needs modeling
|-
| Status || requested / researching / drafting / review / satisfied / declined
|-
| Canonical page || Required when a request becomes satisfied
|-
| Notes || Ambiguity, overlap, duplicate risk, sensitive-topic considerations
|}


== Completed corpus expansion — historical note ==

The numbered concept-wave program (Waves 0–14) concluded in the '''2026-09-19 source snapshot''' at 232 Core concepts and 696 Core/Simple/Technical reader surfaces. These are historical repository-audit counts, not live deployment verification or a promise that coverage is complete. Research dossiers, per-wave context captures, compilations and audits are recoverable in Git history rather than kept as a second public operational index.

New coverage proceeds through independently justified requests and current source review, not through extending the old wave-numbering scheme. Existing requests below retain their individual lifecycle records.

'''Source audit, 2026-11-19:''' All 51 authored <code>Knowledge request</code> invocations were compared against the repository's <code>Main/</code> title inventory at <code>05db31fa</code>. The 32 requests marked <code>satisfied</code> each identify an existing canonical Main-space source page; none of the 17 <code>requested</code> or 2 <code>researching</code> entries has a Main source file under its exact proposed title. This is title-level repository evidence, '''not''' verification of scientific quality, coverage completeness, live wiki publication or broader topic overlap. No request statuses were changed. Twenty <code>source_leads</code> fields mention retired numbered Wave context or compilation records: treat those mentions as historical Git provenance, not live wiki page references. Retain original source leads until their specific historical references are checked, rather than fabricating present-day links.

